.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt SRV 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm srv
.Nd serve a directory or the output of a command over HTTP
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl i Ar duration
.Op Fl t Ar rate
.Op Ar directory
.Nm
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl i Ar duration
.Op Fl t Ar rate
.Op Fl -content-type Ar type
.Fl c Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl h | Fl -help
.Nm
.Fl V | Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
starts an HTTP server that serves the files in
.Ar directory ,
or in the current working directory if no directory is given.
It listens on all IPv4 and IPv6 interfaces on
.Ar port ,
which defaults to 8000.
.Pp
A request for a directory serves its
.Pa index.html
if one exists, and otherwise returns a generated listing of the directory.
A request for a directory without a trailing slash is redirected to the
same path with a trailing slash.
The
.Ql ..\&
and
.Ql .\&
path segments are ignored, so files outside the current directory cannot be
reached.
.Pp
With
.Fl c ,
.Nm
instead runs
.Ar command
for every request and sends its standard output as the response, while the
command is still running.
Nothing is run at startup, and
.Cm HEAD
requests do not run the command.
When the client disconnects,
.Nm
stops the command with
.Dv SIGTERM ,
and with
.Dv SIGKILL
if it has not exited after two seconds.
The command is run directly, not through a shell, and its standard error goes
to the standard error of
.Nm .
.Pp
Only
.Cm GET
and
.Cm HEAD
requests are supported.
The content type is chosen from the file name extension.
Each connection is handled on its own thread and closed after the response.
.Pp
Each request is logged to standard output in the same format as
Python's
.Ql http.server
module.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl p , Fl -port Ar port
Listen on
.Ar port
instead of 8000.
.It Fl i , Fl -idle Ar duration
Stop the server when no request has been made and no connection has been
open for
.Ar duration .
A plain number is a number of seconds.
Units can be given as in
.Ql 30s ,
.Ql 10m ,
.Ql 1h
or
.Ql 1h 30m .
.It Fl t , Fl -throttle Ar rate
Limit the bandwidth to simulate a slow connection.
The limit is shared by all connections, as on a real network link.
.Ar rate
is either a preset, which also delays each response, or a number of bits
per second with an optional
.Ql k ,
.Ql m
or
.Ql g
suffix, such as
.Ql 500k
or
.Ql 1.5m .
The presets are:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "edge" -compact
.It 56k
56 kbit/s, 150 ms latency
.It edge
240 kbit/s, 400 ms latency
.It 3g
750 kbit/s, 300 ms latency
.It 4g
9 Mbit/s, 100 ms latency
.El
.It Fl c , Fl -command Ar command Op Ar argument ...
Serve the output of
.Ar command
instead of a directory.
Everything after
.Fl c
belongs to the command, so
.Fl c
must be the last option.
.It Fl -content-type Ar type
The content type of the command output.
The default is
.Ql text/plain; charset=utf-8 .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
With
.Fl c ,
the command inherits the environment of
.Nm
with these variables added:
.Bl -tag -width "SRV_METHOD"
.It Ev SRV_METHOD
The request method,
.Ql GET .
.It Ev SRV_PATH
The decoded request path, such as
.Ql /some file .
.It Ev SRV_QUERY
The query string without the leading
.Ql \&? ,
as sent.
.It Ev SRV_CLIENT
The address of the client.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
runs until it is interrupted, or exits 0 when the
.Fl i
duration has passed without requests.
It exits 1 if the server cannot be started, for example because
.Ar port
is already in use, and 2 if the arguments are invalid.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Serve the current directory on port 8000:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv
.Pp
Serve the current directory on port 9000:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv -p 9000
.Pp
Serve the
.Pa public
directory:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv public
.Pp
Serve on port 8000 and stop after ten minutes without requests:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv -i 10m
.Pp
Serve the latest commits of the current Git repository:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv -c git log --oneline -20
.Pp
Stream a log file to the browser as it grows:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv -c tail -f app.log
.Pp
Use a shell for pipelines and variables:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv -c sh -c 'echo \(dqYou asked for $SRV_PATH\(dq'
.Pp
Serve on port 9000 as if over a 3G connection:
.Pp
.Dl $ srv -t 3g -p 9000
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr python3 1
.Sh CAVEATS
With
.Fl c ,
the response starts before the command has finished, so the status is always
200, even if the command fails.
A failing exit status is reported on the standard error of
.Nm .
.Pp
.Nm
has no access control and serves every file in the directory, or the output
of the command, to anyone who can reach the port.
Do not run it on untrusted networks.
